  "version": 1,
  "title": "A First Look Through NASA’s Roman Telescope",
  "source": {
    "name": "NASA · 英文导读",
    "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-invites-media-public-to-view-roman-telescopes-first-images/",
    "published": "2026-11-17"
  },
  "level": "B1",
  "paragraphs": [
    "NASA has announced when the public can see the first images from the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope. The agency plans to show them on December 7 during a live broadcast from a scientific meeting in San Francisco. NASA will also put the images on its website at the same time, so people can examine them even if they are not attending the meeting.",
    "The release follows months of preparation. Roman launched on August 30 and traveled toward a point in space called L2, about a million miles from Earth. During the trip, the team switched on the telescope’s systems and checked that its instruments were ready for science. In this case, taking flight was the start of the work needed to produce useful images.",
    "Roman is designed to study large areas of the sky with infrared light. These broad views will help researchers look for patterns across many objects. NASA says the mission will study dark matter, dark energy, and planets that orbit other stars. The first images give people a chance to see what the telescope can do before its wider surveys are complete.",
    "NASA is also inviting people to claim a pixel in one of the first images. A pixel is a tiny part of a digital picture. After the image is public, participants can look up their pixel and get a digital card with information about it. The activity gives each person a small part of a much bigger picture to explore."
  ],
